Pieces of the PC's guest sandbox that other parts of the build add
(docs/HOSTING.md, "Other people's drives on the PC").

linux/pc-host/install.sh runs every *.sh here, as root, after it has
installed the guest units, the slice, the polkit rule and run-guest and
reloaded systemd. They run in name order (LC_ALL=C), so name each with a
number first: 20-backup.sh runs before 30-export.sh. `install.sh --uninstall`
runs them again with --uninstall, in the reverse order, before it takes its
own parts away.

Each piece gets these in its environment:

  OWNER        the user marble-host runs as (bryanmin)
  OWNER_HOME   that user's home
  OPT          /opt/marble: bin, releases and ms-playwright, the owner's
  ETC          /etc/marble-guests: each drive's settings, the owner's, 700
  RELEASE_DIR  the marble-drive folder install.sh was run from (the owner's
               current release, or a checkout)

and in a test, MARBLE_INSTALL_ROOT (a folder standing in for /) and
MARBLE_SYSTEMCTL (a stand-in for systemctl), which a piece should honour as
install.sh does.

A piece must be safe to run again, must print no secret, and must exit
non-zero when it fails: install.sh stops there. Anything root runs belongs
outside /opt/marble, which the owner can write.
